Introduction
Evaluating the quality of natural language gener-
ation (NLG) is inherently challenging due to the
subjective nature of such tasks, where the criteria
for high-quality output can vary based on context
and audience. While human evaluation remains a
common method for assessing generated text, it is
also time-consuming. Recently, researchers (Liu
et al., 2023; Chan et al., 2023; Zheng et al., 2023a)
have turned to large language models (LLMs) as
evaluators, noting their impressive alignment with
human preferences in text assessment.
However, studies (Koo et al., 2023; Panickssery
et al., 2024) have revealed that LLMs exhibit biases,
such as a preference for text generated by the mod-
