2.1
Consistency Formula
Given two models, mi and mj, and a model serving
as a judge mjudge, running a contest between that
pair of models consists of asking the same question
to both models and having the judge pick the best
answer (see Section 2.2 for details on the process
of running an individual contest). A matchup ij is
the set of all contests between models mi and mj,
evaluated by the same judge mjudge.
If the judge’s aggregated preferences for a
matchup yields a probability of winning pij to mi,
we can model the matchup with a Bernoulli distri-
bution of parameter pij and variance pij · (1 −pij).
This variance is inversely correlated with the con-
sistency with which the judge selects a model as
the winner: it approaches 0.25 if both models are
equally likely to win and tends toward 0 if the same
model’s answer is always preferred.
Given a set of matchups M between models, if
we denote nij as the number of contests between
models mi and mj, we can compute the average
variance across matchups using Equation 1.
Var(mjudge, M) =
P
